rounds:
  - round: 1
    found: 3
    verified: 2
    detail: >-
      Five finders, three candidates, two confirmed. (1) push_code_point discarded the hex digits
      take_radix had consumed when the code point named no character (a UTF-16 surrogate, a \U
      past 0x10FFFF), emitting only the two-byte marker where bash keeps the six-byte literal -
      verified by tokenizing both shapes and comparing with a real bash. Important, not blocker:
      Chrome never emits a surrogate escape. (2) Command.saw_data_flag was redundant with
      !data.is_empty(), since -d '' still pushes an empty element. Refuted: UC-6 quoting two
      example refusal wordings and the format refusal string - a use case states the wire-visible
      contract as the same file already does for the import envelope; the verifier noted the
      doc/v1 note's self-description ("not their wording") was imprecise, corrected in round 2.
  - round: 2
    found: 5
    verified: 1
    detail: >-
      Re-review of 9ed23e5 as unreviewed code. Correctness compared the fix byte-for-byte with a
      real bash; altitude confirmed the reworded doc/v1 note. One nit confirmed: take_radix
      accumulated the value by hand in lockstep with collecting the same digits, where
      u32::from_str_radix over the buffer computes the identical value. Four refuted: a
      per-escape Vec allocation (measured ~30 ns per escape over a million, bounded by the store
      cap, the same growth the tokenizer pays per byte); the ansi_c_quoted closing sentence
      (incomplete, not false - push_code_point's own comment carries the fact); the tuple return
      and the \x arm's discarded digits (taste).
  - round: 3
    found: 3
    verified: 2
    detail: >-
      Re-review of a9d9f20. Correctness checked from_str_radix against the old accumulator over
      all 256 byte values. Two nits confirmed: the octal arm still accumulated by hand beside the
      shared take_radix (the verifier noting the helper's no-digits-means-no-escape rule does not
      apply to octal, whose first digit the outer match had consumed); and low_byte's
      u8::try_from(value & 0xff).unwrap_or(u8::MAX) carried a fallback a mask to eight bits can
      never reach. Refuted: the from_utf8 .ok() fallback in take_radix - the idiomatic panic-free
      shape under the pinned unwrap_used lint on a genuinely radix-generic reader.
  - round: 4
    found: 2
    verified: 0
    detail: >-
      Re-review of e295f23, which peeks for an octal digit before consuming and reads all one to
      three digits through take_radix, and reads the low byte with to_le_bytes()[0]. Correctness
      traced the guard's fallthrough for \0, \7, \77, \777, \0z and \8 (which stays the
      literal \8 as before); altitude re-matched the doc/v1 entry list against the docs in the
      diff. Two candidates, both refuted: the octal-digit predicate spelled once as a byte range
      and once as is_digit(8) (equivalent over all 256 bytes; extracting a helper for a one-line
      range check is the abstraction-forcing the rubric excludes), and the guard arm's if let with
      no else (None is unreachable after the peek, every alternative spelling adds dead code or a
      panic path, and it is the shape round 2 already refuted on the \x arm). Verdict PASS.
